<% content_for :title, "Budget" %>

<%# ** THE BUDGET PAGE IS EVERY CATEGORY, AND ONE OF THEM IS OPEN (two-shapes spec §4). **

    Henry, 2026-09-05: "Expanded should just show all rules and suggestions. The new rule thing
    should be a form we go to." So the page is three tiles and one list: a row per EXPENSE category,
    ruled ones in give-way order and rule-less ones after, and the open one shows its rules, the
    suggestions the engine has for it, and one button to write another.

    ** FIVE PANELS DIED HERE (§7). ** `_type_overview` (its three figures are the tiles' segmented
    bar), `_structural_check` (its three lines ARE the three tiles), `_fill_order` and
    `_category_group` (folded into the row), `_not_filling` (every expense category is in the list
    now, so a rule on a category that holds nothing has a row of its own rather than a band saying
    it has none) and `_suggestions` (every suggestion is inside the category it is about). Not one
    figure was dropped: each of them is somewhere on this page, closer to the thing it is about.

    THE DECLARATION FORM SURVIVES, behind the income tile's "change" — `?declare=1`. It was
    permanently open under a paragraph of prose, which put a three-field settings form in the middle
    of the one screen that is about rules. %>
<%# ** THE HEADER'S "New rule" BUTTON IS DELETED (two-shapes spec §5). ** It went to a bare
    `/budgets/new`, whose whole purpose was the owner PICKER — a select asking which category the
    rule was for. The form is per category now (`/budgets/new?category_id=`), titled "New rule for
    Groceries", with that category's items in its select and that category's suggestions above step
    1; there is nothing for a category-less door to open. The door that replaced it is inside each
    category's own panel, one click from the rules it will sit beside, and it is the same door a
    suggestion's "Write it →" goes through. `BudgetsController::NEW_NEEDS_A_CATEGORY` turns anyone
    still holding the old URL back to this page and says so. %>
<%= page_header(title: "Budget", subtitle: "The rules that claim your money") %>

<div class="space-y-6">
  <%# §3.5'S ONE QUESTION, AT THE TOP OF THE PAGE BECAUSE NOTHING BELOW IT IS TRUE YET. The
      declaration that produced it has not been written — the panel and the form both say so — and
      the figures underneath are still computed on the OLD period. Rendered off an ivar rather than
      through the presenter: the presenter is this page's reading of what IS, and this is a pending
      submission that may never become one. See BudgetPageController#offer_scaling. %>
  <%= render "cadence_confirm", change: @cadence_change if @cadence_change %>

  <%= render "tiles", presenter: @presenter %>

  <%# THE FORM, ONLY WHEN ASKED FOR — or when a submission was refused, in which case it is holding
      what the user typed and the errors, and hiding it would throw both away. %>
  <% if @presenter.declaring? %>
    <section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded px-6 py-5" data-declaration>
      <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-4">Your period and income</h2>
      <%= render "declaration_form", presenter: @presenter %>
    </section>
  <% end %>

  <% if @presenter.no_categories? %>
    <%= render "empty" %>
  <% else %>
    <%# THE LIST, AND IT IS WHAT `PATCH /budget/reorder` REWRITES. The drag controller reads the
        order back off the rows at drop; the ▲▼ on each draggable row carry the same list already
        swapped, so the order is reachable with no JavaScript at all.

        `category_list` IS THE SECOND CONTROLLER ON THE SAME ELEMENT: the expand/collapse and the
        drag are two behaviours over one list of rows, and splitting them onto two elements would
        mean one of them binding to a wrapper the other does not own. %>
    <section class="space-y-2"
             data-category-list
             data-controller="app--budget-page--reorder app--budget-page--category-list"
             data-action="dragover->app--budget-page--reorder#over
                          drop->app--budget-page--reorder#drop">
      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline sm:justify-between gap-1 sm:gap-4 px-1">
        <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">Your categories</h2>
        <%# ** THE LIST IS PRIORITY ORDER AND THE COPY SAYS WHAT THAT BUYS (fix round MAJOR-1). **
            The order on screen is the number the arrows write; the give-way order Home draws is that
            number UNDER the rule type, which no control here can reach. So the sentence says both:
            what dragging decides (the tie-break inside a kind), what it cannot decide (the kinds'
            own order), and where the combined answer is shown. %>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 sm:max-w-md sm:text-right">
          Drag to set which gives way first among rules of the same kind — choice always gives way
          before usage, usage before bills. Home shows them in that give-way order.
        </p>
      </div>

      <% rows = @presenter.category_rows %>
      <% reorderable = @presenter.reorderable_rows %>
      <% rows.each do |row| %>
        <%= render "category_row", row: row, reorderable: reorderable, presenter: @presenter %>
      <% end %>

      <% form_data = { app__budget_page__reorder_target: "form" } %>
      <%= form_with(url: budget_page_reorder_path, method: :patch, class: "hidden", data: form_data) do %>
        <%# One hidden `category_ids[]` per draggable row, appended by reorder_controller at drop. %>
      <% end %>
    </section>
  <% end %>
</div>
